::: {.doc-subject}

Add a d-dimensional hypercube graph Q(d) to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L9779-L9904){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_hypercube_graph(
    d,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Hypercube graph](../assets/graphs/add-hypercube-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three hypercube graphs side by side for d=1, 2, and 3: a single edge, a square, and a cube drawn in perspective. Each dimension doubles the graph, joining every node to its copy, so the d-cube has 2^d nodes and every node has d neighbors." width="100%"}

The hypercube has one node for every string of `d` bits, and two nodes are joined when
their strings differ in exactly one bit. It has `2^d` nodes and `d * 2^(d - 1)` edges,
every node has degree `d`, and the shortest-path distance between two nodes is the
number of bits in which they differ (their Hamming distance), so the diameter is `d`.
`Q(1)` is a single edge, `Q(2)` a square, and `Q(3)` the cube.

Each node is labelled with its bit string (e.g. `"0110"`), and nodes are created in
binary counting order, from `"00...0"` to `"11...1"`. In a directed graph every edge
points from the string with a `0` to the one with a `1` in the differing bit. The
hypercube is appended without touching or connecting to existing nodes, and the whole
construction is recorded as a single step.

Hypercubes are useful for:

- modeling interconnection networks for parallel computers and distributed hash tables,
  where each node links to the nodes one bit-flip away
- state spaces of `d` on/off switches, where an edge is a single flip (e.g. Boolean
  network or genotype spaces)
- test cases for highly symmetric, bipartite, regular graphs with known distances
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[d]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Dimension (number of bits). Must be at least `1`. The node count doubles with each
    dimension, so `d=20` already gives about a million nodes.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),
    no `type` attribute is set.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), each node is labelled with its bit string. Because these labels
    don't depend on the existing nodes, adding a second labelled hypercube of the same
    dimension raises `ValueError` (labels are unique); pass `label=False` for the second
    one. If `False`, nodes are created without labels.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge. If `None` (default),
    no `rel` attribute is set.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `d=` is less than `1`.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_grid_graph`) 

:   Add a grid graph in any number of dimensions.

[](`~cartesian_product`) 

:   Join pairs that move in one graph at a time.

[](`~layout_bfs`) 

:   Place the nodes in layers by distance from a start node.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

The hypercube `Q(d)` has a node for each string of `d` bits, and joins two strings that
differ in one bit. The cube `Q(3)` has `2^3 = 8` nodes, each with `3` neighbors, for `12`
edges, and its diameter is `3`:

::: {#4525cf12 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph().add_hypercube_graph(d=3)

graph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges(), graph.diameter()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(8, 12, 3)
```
:::
:::


The labels are the bit strings. The neighbors of `"000"` are the strings with a single
`1`, and the distance between two nodes is the number of bits that differ, here all three:

::: {#37be3793 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
neighbors = graph.get_neighbors("000")

(
    sorted(graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in neighbors),
    graph.shortest_path_length("001", "110"),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(['001', '010', '100'], 3)
```
:::
:::


### Layers by the number of ones

Laid out in layers by distance from `"000"`, each layer holds the strings with the same
number of `1` bits, and every edge joins two neighboring layers. The cube on the left has
layers of `1`, `3`, `3` and `1`, and the four-dimensional cube on the right, with `16`
nodes and `32` edges, has layers of `1`, `4`, `6`, `4` and `1`:

::: {layout-ncol="2"}

::: {#a42b82b3 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
graph.layout_bfs(source="000").plot(
    layout="pos", label_by="label", width=320, height=320, legend=False
)
```

